## Task 5.1 – Four Ways to Reach an Object (3 Marks)

A Cart object can be accessed directly, through a pointer, through a reference, or through dynamic memory allocation.

### Complete Program

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

class Cart {
    string items[10];
    double prices[10];
    int n = 0;
    double discount = 0;

public:
    Cart& add(const string& item, double price) {
        if (n < 10) {
            items[n] = item;
            prices[n] = price;
            n++;
        }

        return *this;
    }

    Cart& setDiscount(double discount) {
        this->discount = discount;
        return *this;
    }

    double total() const {
        double t = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            t += prices[i];
        }

        return t * (100 - discount) / 100;
    }
};

int main() {
    // (a) Direct access
    Cart c1;
    c1.add("Rice", 60);
    c1.add("Dal", 120);

    cout << "Direct: " << c1.total() << endl;

    // (b) Access through pointer
    Cart c2;
    Cart* p = &c2;

    p->add("Oil", 180);

    cout << "Pointer: " << p->total() << endl;

    // (c) Access through reference
    Cart c3;
    Cart& r = c3;

    r.add("Milk", 50);

    cout << "Reference: " << r.total() << endl;

    // (d) Dynamic object using new and delete
    Cart* p4 = new Cart;

    p4->add("Bread", 40);

    cout << "Dynamic: " << p4->total() << endl;

    delete p4;

    // Method chaining using this pointer
    Cart c5;

    c5.add("Rice", 60)
      .add("Dal", 120)
      .setDiscount(10);

    cout << "Chaining: " << c5.total() << endl;

    return 0;
}
```

### Expected Output

```text
Direct: 180
Pointer: 180
Reference: 50
Dynamic: 40
Chaining: 162
```

### Explanation

**(a) Direct access**

```cpp
c1.total();
```

The object is accessed directly using its name.

**(b) Pointer access**

```cpp
p->total();
```

The pointer stores the address of the Cart object. The `->` operator is used to access its members.

**(c) Reference access**

```cpp
r.total();
```

The reference is another name for the existing Cart object.

**(d) Dynamic object**

```cpp
Cart* p4 = new Cart;
delete p4;
```

The object is created dynamically using `new` and released using `delete`.

### Use of the this Pointer

```cpp
return *this;
```

`this` is a pointer to the current object. `*this` refers to the current object itself.

Returning `*this` by reference allows method chaining.

```cpp
this->discount = discount;
```

The left side refers to the class data member. The right side refers to the function parameter.

