## Task 5.2 – Change the Requirement (2 Marks)

The app now keeps an array of pointers to Cart objects for all active customers. The following program finds the cart with the highest total using indexing.

### Complete Program

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Cart {
    double amount;

public:
    Cart(double a) : amount(a) {}

    double total() const {
        return amount;
    }
};

int main() {
    Cart c1(250);
    Cart c2(500);
    Cart c3(350);

    Cart* carts[3] = {&c1, &c2, &c3};

    int highest = 0;

    for (int i = 1; i < 3; i++) {
        if (carts[i]->total() > carts[highest]->total()) {
            highest = i;
        }
    }

    cout << "Highest cart total: "
         << carts[highest]->total() << endl;

    return 0;
}
```

### Expected Output

```text
Highest cart total: 500
```

### Explanation

The array stores the addresses of three Cart objects. The loop compares their totals and stores the index of the highest cart.

### Complexity Analysis

- Time complexity: O(n), because each cart is checked once.
- Space complexity: O(1) extra space, because only the index is stored.

