## Task 5.3 – Compare (2 Marks)

### Comparison of Four Ways

| Method | Safety | Lifetime | When to use |
|---|---|---|---|
| Direct object | Safe and simple | Until the scope ends | Normal object usage |
| Pointer | Can be null; must be handled carefully | Depends on the pointed object | When address-based access is needed |
| Reference | Cannot normally be null and cannot be reseated | Same lifetime as referenced object | When an alias is required |
| Dynamic pointer | Requires memory management | Until `delete` is called | When dynamic lifetime is required |

### Why is this a Pointer?

The `this` pointer stores the address of the current object on which a member function is called.

For example:

```cpp
c.add("Rice", 60);
```

Inside `add()`, `this` points to object `c`.

### What Does *this Refer To?

`*this` dereferences the `this` pointer and refers to the current object itself.

Returning `*this` allows the same object to be used in consecutive function calls.

