## Explain What Happens If add() Returns Cart Instead of Cart&

If `add()` returns `Cart` by value, it returns a copy of the current object instead of a reference to the original object.

During method chaining, subsequent function calls operate on temporary copies. Therefore, modifications made to these copies may not affect the original Cart object.

Returning `Cart&` avoids unnecessary copies and allows method chaining to modify the same original object.

