# ALTERNATIVE CONCEPT AND COMPLEXITY ANALYSIS

## Alternative: std::vector<Cart> Instead of an Array of Pointers

If the carts are stored as objects in a `std::vector<Cart>`, the highest total can be found using indexing.

### Program

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Cart {
    double amount;

public:
    Cart(double a) : amount(a) {}

    double total() const {
        return amount;
    }
};

int main() {
    vector<Cart> carts;

    carts.emplace_back(250);
    carts.emplace_back(500);
    carts.emplace_back(350);

    int highest = 0;

    for (int i = 1; i < carts.size(); i++) {
        if (carts[i].total() > carts[highest].total()) {
            highest = i;
        }
    }

    cout << "Highest cart total: "
         << carts[highest].total() << endl;

    return 0;
}
```

### Expected Output

```text
Highest cart total: 500
```

### Complexity Comparison

| Feature | Array of pointers | vector<Cart> |
|---|---|---|
| Find highest total | O(n) | O(n) |
| Extra search space | O(1) | O(1) |
| Storage | One pointer per element | Objects stored directly |
| Object copying | Pointer copies | Object copies when copied |
| Memory usage | Pointer array + separate objects | Vector storage for objects |
| Access | `carts[i]->total()` | `carts[i].total()` |
| Pointer stability | Depends on pointed object lifetime | Vector growth may invalidate pointers/references to elements |

### What Happens When the Vector Grows?

When a `std::vector<Cart>` grows beyond its current capacity, it may allocate a larger memory block and move or copy its objects into the new block.

Therefore, pointers, references and iterators to elements of the vector may become invalid after reallocation.

### Conclusion

Both methods require O(n) time to find the highest cart total. An array of pointers stores one pointer for each cart, while a vector of objects stores the actual Cart objects. A vector of objects is generally simpler and avoids separate object allocation, but its growth may invalidate pointers and references to its elements.

